course: dictionary
lesson: 3476
title: Spotting a fallacy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A fallacy is a false idea that many people believe, or a mistake in reasoning that makes an argument wrong, even when it sounds convincing. Ask yourself: does the reason really prove the point, or does it just sound good?
explain: «Everyone does it, so it must be right.» Many people doing something does not prove it is right. The sentence sounds strong, but the reason is weak, so it is a fallacy.
explain: «The gambler's fallacy» is thinking a result is "due" because it has not happened for a while. A coin that has landed on heads five times still has a 50-50 chance of tails next. Use the swap test: put your idea into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A fallacy is a false idea, or a mistake in reasoning that makes an argument wrong.
fact: «Fallacy» is a noun. The plural is «fallacies».
fact: A fallacy can sound convincing, so check the reason, not just how the sentence sounds.
fact: The «gambler's fallacy» is thinking a result is "due" after it has not happened for a while.
fact: Spotting fallacies helps you judge news and adverts.
exercise: «Everyone does it, so it must be right.» What is the fallacy here? | Thinking that five is the best number | Thinking something is right just because many people do it | *Thinking a coin always lands on tails | Thinking a fact must be false
exercise: Sam says, "The coin has landed on heads five times, so tails is due now." What is the mistake? | Coins never land on tails | *It is the gambler's fallacy: each toss is still 50-50 | The coin will definitely land on heads again
exercise: Which word means a mistake in reasoning that makes an argument wrong, even when it sounds convincing? | *Fallacy | Question | Fact | Puzzle
test: The gambler's fallacy is thinking a result is what? | *Due, because it has not happened for a while | Always the same as last time | Certain to happen
test: The sunk cost fallacy is spending more because you have already spent. Is that a good reason to spend more? | *No, money spent is gone whatever you do next | Yes, because you have already spent | Yes, if you feel sad about it
exam: Which statement is a fallacy? | Money you have already spent is gone | *Two lucky boxes in a row mean the next box is more likely to be lucky | Each coin toss is 50-50, whatever came before
